{"actor": "cc", "at": "2026-10-10T22:09:42", "field": "cast_checks", "kind": "field", "value": {"assumption_check": "THREE. (1) That his answers live in the three places measured (decisions/, ticket decisions[], chat) — the open question open-de25b82c3ced asks him to name the population; the verb is built the same whichever he names. (2) That a ruling's the_ruling_verbatim is an answer to a question CC put — read per ruling from kind + confirmation_verbatim, with the no-question-shape fallback a decision line. (3) That 'stable' has arrived — carried by depends_on, never judged here.", "builder_check": "A Haiku builder needs, and the ticket answers: WHERE is the verb? cairn/tools/question/question.py beside open_question (:152) and answer (:184); migrate(ticket, question, answer, *, answered_at, source, raised_by, root=None) builds the same record dict as open_question, sets date=answered_at[:10], resolved=True, answer, answered_by='Akien, migrated from <source> by caller class <class>', answered_at, spawned=[], source; refuses when any record with the same ticket+source exists (idempotent); calls _write once and _link_to_ticket once. WHAT is a ruling's question? confirmation_verbatim's question when kind says confirmation, else 'what does the spec say about <id>?'; the answer is the_ruling_verbatim verbatim. WHAT is a decision line's question? the decision text with 'is it that ' prefixed and '?' appended; the answer is the phrase quoted in by, or the whole by when nothing is quoted. WHERE do the 573 go? doc['distinctions'] = [a for {q,a} if q=='distinctions']; the 52 leftovers to doc['cast_checks'][q] when absent, else dropped as duplicates of the cast_checks already there (measured: 114 tickets carry intra-ticket duplicates); doc['questions'] keeps only open-* strings; write via door.write(verb='cast'). HOW is it run? python3 -m cairn.tools.question.migrate --dry-run prints the counts; without it writes. WHAT NOT to touch: decisions/, the ruling files, open_question/answer/rebind.", "collision_check": "Over the whys. 9adc6fddf185 (rulings retired, decisions/ read-only and citable) — the migration copies with source= and writes nothing there. Question charter edge (c) ('answered' is a field, not a judgement) — a migrated answer is his verbatim words; the field means exactly that. Law 5 — one corpus at one address. The artifact door — question._write rides it. No exclusive-and-terminal collision.", "falsifier_check": "DONE when (1) every ruling under decisions/ with the_ruling_verbatim stands ALSO as a resolved open-* record bound to the ticket the ruling names (or to the ruling's own id when it names none), question = the ruling's question as put, answer = his words verbatim, answered_at = the ruling's date, source = the ruling path — decisions/ itself untouched (read-only and citable per 9adc6fddf185); (2) every ticket decisions[] line whose by names Akien stands as a resolved open-* bound to that ticket, answer = the quoted phrase, and the line gains 'question: open-…' beside by; (3) the 573 inline {q:'distinctions', a} entries live under a distinctions[] field on the same ticket and the 52 cast_check leftovers under cast_checks — questions[] on every ticket then holds open-* ids only, measured by a census tooth over the live corpus in cairn/tools/question/proofs/ (D15: the proof reads a fixture corpus; the live census is the verdict's instrument); (4) the migration is one door verb, question.migrate(...), journaled per record through the artifact door, idempotent (a second run writes 0 records); (5) the counts before and after — questions outside the corpus: (a) 94, (b) 48; after: 0 and 0 — are on the verdict by instrument. RED if a migrated answer differs by one byte from its source, if decisions/ is written, or if a question is opened with a today-date instead of its source date. WRONG INTENT if his answer to the population question narrows it.", "missing_check": "ONE point not wrapped, on purpose: chat-log answers with 